1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Yuwen Hu 宇文護 (513–572), zi Sabao 薩保. Northern Zhou minister and writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yuwen Hu was a Xianbei. His ancestral home was Wuchuan 武川 in Dai commandary 代郡 (modern Wuchuan, Inner Mongolia). His grandfather was Yuwen Gong 宇文肱 (d. 526), who was posthumously granted the title Emperor De 德皇帝. His father was Yu
2 CONC wen Hao 宇文顥 (fl. 520–525), an elder brother of Yuwen Tai 宇文泰 (507–556). Yuwen Tai's third son Yuwen Jue 宇文覺 (542–557) formally established the Northern Zhou dynasty in 557. From an early age Yuwen Hu was upright and had a strong s
2 CONC ense of moral purpose. His grandfather was particularly fond of him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yuwen Hu was eleven when his father died. He and his uncles decided to join the forces of Northern Wei general Ge Rong 葛榮 (d. 528). When Ge Rong was defeated, they moved to Jinyang 晉陽 (southwest of modern Tai-yuan 太原, Shanxi). In 
2 CONC 531, Yuwen Hu moved from Jinyang to Pingliang 平涼 (modern Pingliang, Gansu). At that time, Yuwen Hu's grandfather entrusted him with household duties because all his brothers and paternal cousins were still relatively young. Yuwen 
2 CONC Hu took his duties seriously but was not overly strict with his relatives. Yuwen Tai remarked about him, “This child's moral purpose and magnanimity are like mine.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Yuwen Tai served in the Western Wei court (535–556), he and Yuwen Hu joined with other generals in defeating the Northern Wei forces in several battles. Yuwen Hu helped to establish the Western Wei dynasty, and he was enfeoff
2 CONC ed as Earl of Shuichi county. In 535, Yuwen Hu was promoted to senior recorder for comprehensive duty and General Who Punishes the Enemy. On numerous occasions he accompanied Yuwen Tai on military campaigns, and his meritorious mi
2 CONC litary service won him several promotions. He was appointed General Who Defends the East and grand protector. In 542, he was granted the titles chariot and horse general and unequaled in honor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 556 Yuwen Tai became gravely ill on an inspection tour to the west. He sent messengers to summon Yuwen Hu to see him. When Yuwen Hu arrived at Yuwen Tai's sickbed, Tai said to Hu, “Given my apperance and condition, I don't thin
2 CONC k I can make it. Your brothers are still young, and rebels have not been quelled. Affairs of the realm are your responsibility. I hope you will fully exert yourself to fulfill my wishes.” Yuwen Hu vowed to carry out his instructio
2 CONC ns. After the funeral, Yuwen Hu was able to gain control of both civil and military authority and deposed Emperor Gong 恭 (r. 554–556) of the Western Wei dynasty. In 557, Yuwen Hu assisted Yuwen Tai's third son, Yuwen Jue 宇文覺 (542–
2 CONC 557), to establish the Northern Zhou dynasty. His posthumous title is Emperor Xiaomin 孝閔 (r. 557). Later in the same year, Yuwen Hu deposed Emperor Xiaomin and installed Yuwen Yu 宇文毓 (534–560), the eldest son of Yuwen Tai's second
2 CONC ary wife. This was Emperor Ming 明 (r. 557–560). A few years later, Yuwen Hu poisoned Emperor Ming and helped install Yuwen Yong 宇文邕 (543–578) on the throne. This was Emperor Wu 武 (r. 561–578). During these turbulent years, Yuwen H
2 CONC u arrogated all authority to himself. He also held a number of powerful positions, including commander-in-chief, grand minister of state, and grand preceptor. In 572, Emperor Wu ordered Yuwen Hu executed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Northern Zhou dynasty was first established, Yuwen Hu's mother née Yan and her relatives were held captive by the Northern Qi. Yuwen Hu repeatedly requested to have his mother released but was unsuccessful. The Northern Q
2 CONC i ruler had someone write a letter to Hu on behalf of his mother stating that mother and son had been separated for over thirty years. Now that she was eighty years old she was not expected to live much longer. As a devoted son, Y
2 CONC uwen Hu wrote an equally moving long letter in reply. Before his letter was sent, his mother was released and was able to join her son in Chang'an. She died in 567, three years after their reunion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yuwen Hu's letter to his mother titled “Bao mu Yan ji shu” 報母閻姬書 (A letter replying to my mother Lady Yan) is included in his biography in the ''Zhou shu''. In addition to this letter, Yan Kejun has collected three additional lett
2 CONC ers and a petition “Ju Tan Yan yu Zhou Hongzheng dui lun biao” 舉曇延與周弘正對論表 (Petition recommending that Tan Yan and Zhou Hongzheng engage in a dialogue) in his ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Yu Shiming 余世明. “Bei Zhou xingzao zhi chen—Yuwen Hu shulun” 北周興造 之臣—宇文護述論. Guizhou daxue xuebao (Shehui kexue ban) (1991: 2): 90–94. 
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 152.
2 CONT
