1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Narsi '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Narsi''' or '''Narse''' (Middle Persian: 𐭭𐭥𐭮𐭧𐭩), was an Sasanian nobleman who served as the governor of Kaskar.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT Narsi is first mentioned in 628, he, along with his brother Mah-Adhur Gushnasp, was the son of a certain Jushnas, and an unnamed Ispahbudhan noblewoman, who was the sister of Vistahm and Vinduyih. In 628, during the reign of Ardas
2 CONC hir III, Narsi was granted with Kaskar as a part of his domains,[1] while his brother Mah-Adhur Gushnasp was elected as minister of the empire, and administered the empire greatly.[2][3] One year later, just when stability and pea
2 CONC ce seems to have begun, Shahrbaraz rebelled with a force of 6,000 men,[2] and marched towards Ctesiphon and besieged the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shahrbaraz, however, was unable to capture the city, and then made an alliance with Piruz Khosrow, the leader of the Parsig faction, and the previous minister of the Empire during the reign of Ardashir's father, Kavadh II. He also
2 CONC  made an alliance with Namdar Jushnas, the spahbed of Nemroz.[4] Shahrbaraz, with the aid of these two powerful figures, captured Ctesiphon, and executed Ardashir III, along with Mah-Adhur Gushnasp, the brother of Narsi. Forty day
2 CONC s later, Shahrbaraz was murdered by Farrukh Hormizd, a relative of Narsi, who then made Borandukht, the daughter of Khosrau II, ascend the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a period of coups and revolts, Yazdegerd III was crowned as king of the Sasanian Empire in 632 at Estakhr. With his accession, the civil war in Persia had ended. However, one year later, the Muslim Arabs invaded Persia, and 
2 CONC by 634, were getting close to Ctesiphon, the capital of the Sasanian Empire. Rostam Farrokhzad, a relative of Narsi, then sent him along with Jaban to confront the Arabs at Namaraq, a city near Kufa. Narsi and Jaban, were, however
2 CONC , defeated. Narsi then fled along with his army to his territory at Kaskar. Rostam then ordered Narsi: "[Go] off to your estate and protect it from your enemy and our enemy. Be a man."[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Narsi then met the Arabs at near Kaskar, and was aided by the sons of Vistahm, Tiruyih and Vinduyih,[6] who commanded the flanks of Narsi's army. Shortly before the Sasanian army and the Arab army clashed, Rostam sent Jalinus at t
2 CONC he head of an Armenian army to aid Narsi. However, when he arrived to Kaskar, Narsi was defeated and had already fled. The Arabs then defeated the army of Jalinus.[7] What happened to Narsi afterwards is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Michael G. Morony, Iraq After the Muslim Conquest, 2005. (p. 157)
2 CONT # ARDAŠĪR III, A. Sh. Shahbazi, Encyclopædia Iranica,(11 August 2011).[1]
2 CONT # Pourshariati (2008), p. 179
2 CONT # Pourshariati (2008), p. 180
2 CONT # Pourshariati (2008), p. 212
2 CONT # Shapur Shahbazi (1989), BESṬĀM O BENDŌY
2 CONT # Pourshariati (2008), p. 213
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Pourshariati, Parvaneh (2008). Decline and Fall of the Sasanian Empire: The Sasanian-Parthian Confederacy and the Arab Conquest of Iran. London and New York: I.B. Tauris. ISBN 978-1-84511-645-3.
2 CONT * Shahbazi, A. Sh. (1986). "ARDAŠĪR III". Encyclopaedia Iranica, Vol. II, Fasc. 4. pp. 381–382.
